---
import HelpLayout from '../../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';

/**
 * SEO / compatibility page, same shape and same rules as
 * stronglifts-5x5-tracker.astro. Read that file's header before editing this
 * one; the reasoning is identical.
 *
 * TRADEMARK HYGIENE, binding: FitNotes is someone else's app. Everything here
 * is phrased as compatibility (Badger reads the backup file their app writes),
 * never as affiliation or endorsement, and never as a criticism of their app.
 * FitNotes users like FitNotes. A page that opens by running it down loses the
 * reader in one sentence, and would deserve to.
 *
 * Claims about what the import carries were checked against the app source on
 * 2026-09-05, against lib/features/import/domain/fitnotes_parser.dart and
 * fitnotes_importer.dart:
 *   - categories: recreated by name WITH their FitNotes color, reused when a
 *     category of that name already exists (_doImport step 1)
 *   - exercises: matched by name, then by the reviewed mapping plan, else
 *     created in their original category with the FitNotes exercise type
 *     mapped across (step 2)
 *   - sets: reps, weight, distance, time, comment; both the modern
 *     `workout_entry` schema and the pre-2018 `training_log` one, where the
 *     kg-canonical column is turned back into the unit it was typed in
 *   - body weight: appended to the Body Weight measurement type
 *   - custom measurements: their own types, carrying the unit string
 * There is deliberately no warmup claim: FitNotes does not mark warmups, so
 * the importer has nothing to read and every set arrives as a working set.
 *
 * 2026-09-12: the preview step used to say Badger "detects" the weight unit. It
 * does not, for the schema most people will have. `_parseWorkoutEntry` (the
 * post-2018 table) builds its entries with no storedUnit at all, so the import
 * runs on the kg/lbs control the user sets on the preview screen
 * (`_importWeightUnit`, defaulting to kg). Only `_parseTrainingLog`, the legacy
 * table, reads a per-row unit column. Both this page and
 * /guides/fitnotes-on-iphone now say which is which.
 */

const description =
  'Move to Badger from FitNotes without losing anything. Your .fitnotes backup brings the whole library across: categories, exercises, every set, body weight, and your custom measurements.';

const modified = '2026-09-24';
---

<HelpLayout title="Moving from FitNotes" description={description}>
  <ArticleSchema
    slot="head"
    headline="Moving to Badger from FitNotes"
    description={description}
    dateModified={modified}
    about={['FitNotes', 'Workout tracking']}
    breadcrumb={[
      { name: 'Guides', url: '/guides/' },
      { name: 'Switching to Badger', url: '/guides/switching/' },
    ]}
  />

  <p class="crumb"><a href="/guides/switching/">Switching to Badger</a></p>

  <h1>Moving to Badger from FitNotes</h1>

  <p>
    If you like FitNotes for being fast, free and offline, Badger is all three. Your
    <code>.fitnotes</code> backup brings your whole library across along with your sets. Here is
    exactly what comes with it and what you gain.
  </p>

  <h2 id="import">What the backup file carries across</h2>

  <p>
    A <code>.fitnotes</code> backup is a full database, not a flattened CSV, so far more than your
    sets comes across. Badger reads it on your phone.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Your categories, with their colors.</strong> A category you already have in Badger is
      reused instead of duplicated.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your exercise library, in those categories</strong>, instead of an "Imported" bucket.
      Each exercise keeps its FitNotes type (weight and reps, cardio, and so on), so the logging
      screen shows the right fields.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Every set</strong>, with its date, weight, reps, distance, time, and any comment you
      left on it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Backups from before 2018</strong>, when FitNotes changed its database layout. On those,
      Badger turns the stored kilograms back into the unit you typed, so a pounds log stays in pounds.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Your body weight history</strong>, added to Badger's body weight track.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your custom measurements</strong> (waist, arms, whatever you tracked), each as its own
      measurement type with its unit.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your PRs</strong>, worked out again from the imported history, so your records and
      graphs start out full.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>What does not come across:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Warmups, as warmups.</strong> FitNotes does not mark them, so every set arrives as a
      working set. You can flag warmups yourself afterward.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Routines.</strong> You set programs up in Badger, starting from scratch or from one of
      the built-in programs.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="how">Doing it</h2>

  <p>
    In FitNotes, go to <strong>Settings &rarr; Backup</strong> to write a <code>.fitnotes</code>
    file, move it to the phone you train with, then open <strong>Settings &rarr; Your data &rarr;
    Import from FitNotes</strong> in Badger. Import each backup once. The full steps are in
    <a href="/help/getting-started/#importing">importing from another app</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    On the preview, check the weight unit. Badger assumes kilograms, which is what FitNotes normally
    stores; a pre-2018 backup carries its own unit on each set.
  </p>

  <h2 id="whats-different">What you get that you did not have</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Routines that pre-fill.</strong> Build your program once and each session opens with
      your exercises and last weights in place.
      <a href="/programs/">Ten programs ship built in</a>, including StrongLifts 5x5, GZCLP and PPL.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Gym and machine tracking.</strong> Record which gym and machine a set was done on, with
      PRs kept per machine. <a href="/help/gyms/">How that works</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A plate calculator and a warmup generator</strong> that work from the plates your gym
      owns and round to loads you can make.
    </li>
    <li><strong>A rest timer that starts itself</strong> when you complete a set, set per exercise.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>More graphs.</strong> Estimated 1RM, eleven graphs per exercise, an activity heatmap,
      weekly volume by muscle group, and plateau detection.
      <a href="/help/progress/">What it tracks</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An iPhone version.</strong> Badger is on Google Play and the App Store, so a new phone
      does not mean a new app. <a href="/guides/fitnotes-on-iphone/">FitNotes on iPhone</a> covers
      getting the backup from an Android phone to an iPhone.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>What stays the same: no account, no sign-in, and your log lives on your phone.</p>

  <h2 id="getting-out">Getting back out again</h2>

  <p>
    You can take your log with you if you leave. Every set exports to CSV, and the whole database
    backs up to a file you keep. Routines, exercises and equipment share as <code>.badger</code>
    files, a <a href="/badger-format/">documented format</a> you can read in any text editor.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cost">What it costs</h2>

  <p>
    Nothing: no subscription, no ads, and no cap on routines or history.
    <a href="/guides/workout-tracker-no-subscription/">The no-subscription guide</a> compares what
    free means across the trackers people usually weigh up.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trademark">About the name</h2>

  <p class="fineprint">
    FitNotes is the work of its own developer, and Badger is not affiliated with, endorsed by,
    sponsored by, or connected to it in any way. This page describes compatibility only: Badger reads
    the backup file that FitNotes writes.
  </p>
</HelpLayout>

<style>
  /* The section this page sits in, named above the h1 so a reader landing here
     from a search knows there are sibling pages for the other apps. */
  .crumb {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* Matches the StrongLifts page: a disclaimer should read as one rather than
     competing with the guidance above it. Muted is AA-checked in both themes. */
  .fineprint {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
